2026-05-09  Nail #2: map gen + strategic-scale render
  WHAT: Replaced the placeholder grid with a real procedural
        map. Three new things:
        - world.ts (~140 LOC) Pure data. Mulberry32 RNG.
          Bilinear value-noise (two octaves) + radial continent
          falloff → land/water mask. Sand pass marks any grass
          tile cardinally adjacent to water. Region seeds picked
          on land with min-spacing rejection sampling. Multi-
          source BFS with shuffled neighbor order grows organic
          blobs across all land. Per-region centroid + tile count.
          Placeholder owner colors via golden-ratio hue rotation
          on HSL.
        - mapRender.ts Builds three Pixi Graphics layers from
          a World: terrain (water/sand/grass colors), tint
          (region.color over land at alpha 0.55), borders
          (vector strokes along boundaries between differing
          regions or land-vs-water).
        - main.ts rewired: drop placeholder, instantiate world
          (100x100, 25 regions, seed 1337, TILE_SIZE=16). Same
          camera + input. Crossfade now fades OUT tint + borders
          as zoom goes tactical (was fade IN of grid lines on the
          placeholder). Readout shows region count.
        Deleted: placeholder.ts.
  WHY:  Per the mantra, Nail #2 = the next single aspect:
        strategic-scale terrain + regions, no soldiers yet, per
        port.md's "First milestone" list. Visual style locks the
        engine (lesson #1) so getting the strategic look right
        before adding units is intentional. Still ~210-220 KB gz
        bundle. Typecheck clean, build clean. To be tested on
        phone for thumbs-up before Nail #3 (probably soldier
        sprites + formations to start the tactical aesthetic).

